00:51:57.560 --> 00:51:59.990
Daniil Ankushin | Nethermind: So, yeah, so…

384
00:51:59.990 --> 00:52:04.579
lightclient: Are you saying that you send transactions to peers who can't?

385
00:52:04.850 --> 00:52:06.410
lightclient: Verify them.

386
00:52:06.780 --> 00:52:10.169
lightclient: And then they fail to verify because it's over their limits.

387
00:52:13.180 --> 00:52:19.650
Daniil Ankushin | Nethermind: Yeah, it's just, will be slower, but fewer chance to be included.

388
00:52:20.210 --> 00:52:28.859
lightclient: Right, but I think it's not a good thing to send transactions around the mempool that we know can't get accepted by peers.

389
00:52:29.390 --> 00:52:30.140
lightclient: Like…

390
00:52:30.630 --> 00:52:36.850
lightclient: We need to optimize the mempool a lot from where we are today, and I think adding more rules that

391
00:52:37.120 --> 00:52:41.560
lightclient: Make it not possible to do that is not going to help us.

392
00:52:42.040 --> 00:52:47.220
lightclient: So like the alternative is to have a protocol to communicate what your.

393
00:52:49.310 --> 00:52:51.509
lightclient: You know what your verify limit is.

394
00:52:51.950 --> 00:53:00.210
lightclient: To me, that that feels a little a little heavy, but it's like something we can obviously do. I mean, it's the same type of thing that we've done with storage of history.

395
00:53:00.630 --> 00:53:07.749
lightclient: So, you know, you don't want to be sending a bunch of transactions to peers that can't process them, because then you also don't know

396
00:53:08.020 --> 00:53:16.850
lightclient: If they didn't process them and then you might again send them later if they send you the hashes that they have and they don't have the hash, hashes that you have.

397
00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:25.480
lightclient: So to me, like, without a communication mechanism to say what they're able to verify, like, I think this is…

398
00:53:26.610 --> 00:53:29.550
lightclient: This is adding too much stress to the network.

